WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
C gives developers explicit control over allocation and deallocation.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:13.720
That control is useful, but it also means the program must define who owns each resource, when it is safe to use, and when it should be released.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.300
The language will not infer those ownership rules from the pointer value alone.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.900
A pointer does not automatically record whether it owns memory.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:36.200
The same pointer type can represent an owning reference, a borrowed view, a pointer into an array, a pointer to static storage, a pointer returned by a library, or a temporary pointer used only for one operation.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.980
The ownership meaning comes from the contract, not from the syntax.

7
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.860
A pointer also does not automatically record whether the object it once referenced is still alive.

8
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:53.320
After release, scope exit, reallocation, or ownership transfer, a saved pointer may still contain a value.

9
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.100
Defensive code does not treat that value as proof of validity.

10
00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.140
Ownership describes who is responsible for releasing a resource.

11
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.320
A function that creates an object may return ownership to the caller.

12
00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:08.620
A container may take ownership of inserted elements.

13
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.280
A cleanup routine may consume ownership and make the old reference invalid.

14
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.540
These rules should be easy to find.

15
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.540
Borrowing describes temporary access without responsibility for cleanup.

16
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:28.400
A borrowed pointer may be safe while the owner remains alive and while the callee does not retain it beyond the promised lifetime.

17
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.780
Borrowing becomes risky when a function stores a pointer that was meant only for immediate use.

18
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:40.700
Function contracts should make ownership transfer, borrowing, retention, and cleanup expectations clear.

19
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:52.000
Names, comments, types, and surrounding patterns should tell callers whether they may free a pointer, whether they must free it, whether they may keep it, and when it stops being valid.
